BEGIN;
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '4918ac86-500f-52ae-9cc3-1b1a72c74f33','Celluloid-cylinder delamination','A deterioration of celluloid phonograph cylinders (such as Edison Blue Amberol and other ''indestructible'' types) in which the thin celluloid playing layer shrinks, cracks, or separates from its inner plaster-of-Paris or cardboard core. It is commonly driven by the core absorbing moisture and swelling while the celluloid resists movement, splitting the recorded surface and threatening the grooves.','This is damage to celluloid record cylinders. The thin celluloid surface can shrink, crack, or peel away from the core inside. It often happens when the core soaks up moisture and swells. The cracks can reach the grooves and ruin the recording.','Naming this failure mode lets a conservator identify at-risk cylinders, correct their storage, and prioritize transfer before the grooved surface is lost.','A technician inspects cylinders for cracks or lifting at the ends, controls storage humidity and temperature, handles them only by the ends and inner surface, and prioritizes damaged items for transfer.','Carrier Degradation',ARRAY['Carrier preservation','Carrier metadata','Carrier-based obsolescence','Belt flattening','Archival transfer chain']::text[],ARRAY['Storing celluloid cylinders in damp or fluctuating conditions, which swells the core and cracks the celluloid.','UNSAFE: Handling cold, brittle celluloid cylinders roughly or touching the grooved surface, which can shatter the fragile item; support them only by the inner surface and the end edges.','Assuming celluloid cylinders are truly ''indestructible'' as once marketed and skipping regular inspection.']::text[],ARRAY['Finding lifting and cracks on Blue Amberol cylinders during a collection survey.','Setting stable, cool, moderate-humidity storage to slow core swelling.','Prioritizing delaminating cylinders for urgent transfer.']::text[],'f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='4918ac86-500f-52ae-9cc3-1b1a72c74f33' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Celluloid-cylinder delamination'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '4918ac86-500f-52ae-9cc3-1b1a72c74f33','f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='4918ac86-500f-52ae-9cc3-1b1a72c74f33' AND gt.achievement_id='f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'fa0f022c-d8b5-5470-8923-f9f61a755711','Checksum collision','A checksum collision occurs when two different data inputs produce the identical checksum or hash value under a given algorithm. Because a fixed-length checksum maps a practically unlimited set of inputs onto a finite range of outputs, collisions are mathematically unavoidable in principle; a weak or compromised algorithm such as MD5 or SHA-1 makes them practically findable, undermining the assumption that a matching checksum proves two files are the same.','A checksum is a short code made from a file''s data. A collision is when two different files make the same code. When that happens, a matching code no longer proves the files match. So archives pick strong methods where collisions are very hard to find.','The concept matters because fixity checking assumes a matching checksum means unchanged content; understanding collisions is why archives choose collision-resistant algorithms and treat a match as strong, not absolute, evidence of integrity.','A digital archivist avoids collision-prone algorithms like MD5 or SHA-1 for integrity-critical fixity and prefers SHA-256, so that a matching checksum reliably indicates a file has not been altered or substituted.','Fixity & Integrity',ARRAY['Cryptographic hash','Checksum manifest','Fixity','Data integrity','Content preservation']::text[],ARRAY['Assuming any matching checksum guarantees two files are byte-for-byte identical; a collision in a weak algorithm can defeat this.','Believing collisions are only theoretical; practical collisions have been demonstrated for MD5 and SHA-1.','Confusing an accidental collision, which is astronomically unlikely with a strong hash, with a deliberately engineered one.']::text[],ARRAY['Choosing a hash algorithm for a preservation repository and rejecting MD5 because documented collisions weaken its integrity guarantee.','Investigating why two clearly different files share the same MD5 value during a fixity audit.','Explaining to stakeholders why a repository moved its fixity checks from SHA-1 to SHA-256.']::text[],'f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='fa0f022c-d8b5-5470-8923-f9f61a755711' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Checksum collision'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'fa0f022c-d8b5-5470-8923-f9f61a755711','f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='fa0f022c-d8b5-5470-8923-f9f61a755711' AND gt.achievement_id='f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '6ef3e876-02c5-54f5-8881-22b947d77fe0','Checksum manifest','A checksum manifest is a file that lists every file in a package alongside its computed checksum and relative path, enabling automated verification that all expected files are present and unaltered. In the BagIt packaging format (IETF RFC 8493) it is named manifest-algorithm.txt, for example manifest-sha256.txt, with one line per payload file in the form checksum followed by filepath.','A checksum manifest is a list that pairs each file with its own short check code. Later, software works out each code again and compares it to the list. If every code matches, the files are all there and unchanged. If one differs, that file was damaged or altered.','It provides a portable, machine-readable record of expected files and their fixity values, so a repository can confirm completeness and detect corruption or loss whenever content is stored, moved, or copied.','When receiving or sending a BagIt bag, a technician runs a validation tool that reads the manifest, recomputes each checksum, and reports any missing, extra, or changed files before the content is accepted into the archive.','Fixity & Integrity',ARRAY['Cryptographic hash','Checksum collision','Fixity','BagIt','Content migration']::text[],ARRAY['Treating the manifest as a mere inventory and never actually re-running the checksums to validate it.','Editing files after the manifest is created without regenerating it, so validation falsely reports corruption.','Keeping the manifest only inside the same package with no independent copy, so a single tampering event could alter both.']::text[],ARRAY['Validating an incoming BagIt transfer to confirm no files were lost or corrupted in transit.','Auditing archival storage periodically by recomputing checksums against the stored manifest.','Generating a manifest before writing a preservation copy to LTO tape or a remote repository.']::text[],'f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='6ef3e876-02c5-54f5-8881-22b947d77fe0' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Checksum manifest'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '6ef3e876-02c5-54f5-8881-22b947d77fe0','f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='6ef3e876-02c5-54f5-8881-22b947d77fe0' AND gt.achievement_id='f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'e910b806-94b9-50ff-9bff-572fb6ed397a','Coding history field','The Coding History field is a free-text element in the bext (Broadcast Extension) chunk of a Broadcast Wave Format (BWF) file, defined by EBU Tech 3285, that records the signal chain and each coding or processing stage a recording has passed through. Each line documents one stage, giving coding type such as PCM or analogue, sample rate, word length, mode, and the device or operator, so the file carries a chronological technical provenance.','This is a text note stored inside a Broadcast Wave audio file. It lists each step the sound went through. For example, it records the analog source, the converter, the sample rate, and the bit depth. It gives future users a history of how the file was made.','It preserves technical provenance so anyone handling the file later can trace how it was digitized and processed, supporting authenticity, quality assessment, and correct interpretation during long-term preservation.','When digitizing an analog tape to BWF, an archivist writes a Coding History line describing the playback machine, the analogue-to-digital converter, and the target sample rate and word length, and appends a new line each time the file is re-encoded.','Archival Metadata',ARRAY['Broadcast Wave Format','Content migration','Context information','Content Information','Controlled vocabulary']::text[],ARRAY['Leaving the field blank during digitization, discarding provenance that cannot be reconstructed later.','Overwriting the previous history when re-encoding instead of appending a new stage line.','Recording marketing model names instead of the technical parameters (sample rate, word length, mode) the format expects.']::text[],ARRAY['Documenting the signal chain while transferring a quarter-inch tape to a BWF preservation master.','Reviewing a file''s Coding History to judge whether it was captured at archival resolution.','Passing provenance forward when normalizing a collection of legacy WAV files to BWF.']::text[],'f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='e910b806-94b9-50ff-9bff-572fb6ed397a' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Coding history field'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'e910b806-94b9-50ff-9bff-572fb6ed397a','f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='e910b806-94b9-50ff-9bff-572fb6ed397a' AND gt.achievement_id='f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'aa1c12db-d580-55b2-b742-bb571416baf1','Concentric cracking','Concentric cracking is a pattern of cracks that run in curved arcs roughly parallel to the grooves of a disc recording, seen most often on lacquer (acetate) discs. As the lacquer coating loses plasticizer, embrittles, and shrinks while bonded to a non-shrinking aluminium, glass, or fibre core, the internal stress relieves along circular lines, and the coating can progress to flaking and loss of the sound-carrying layer.','This is cracking that forms in curved lines that follow a record''s grooves. It mostly affects lacquer discs. The coating dries out, shrinks, and pulls against the solid base underneath. That stress splits the surface in circular cracks, and pieces can flake off and take the sound with them.','Recognizing concentric cracking lets an archivist judge how far a lacquer disc has degraded and prioritize urgent transfer before the cracked coating flakes away and the audio is lost for good.','During condition assessment a technician inspects a lacquer disc under raking light for circular cracks, flags cracked discs as at-risk, and handles them flat and gently to avoid dislodging loose coating before digitization.','Media Degradation',ARRAY['Concentric runout','Cut groove','Delamination','Content migration','Content preservation']::text[],ARRAY['UNSAFE: Assuming a degrading lacquer disc is inert; the cellulose-nitrate lacquer is chemically unstable and flammable, so store it cool and dry and follow institutional hazardous-material guidance.','Trying to play a concentrically cracked disc, which can shed coating and further destroy the grooves.','Confusing surface cracking of the coating with a broken base, leading to the wrong handling and transfer approach.']::text[],ARRAY['Triaging a box of 1940s radio transcription discs and separating cracked lacquers for priority digitization.','Documenting crack patterns in a condition report before an audio transfer.','Deciding a fragile cracked disc needs specialist touchless optical scanning rather than stylus playback.']::text[],'f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='aa1c12db-d580-55b2-b742-bb571416baf1' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Concentric cracking'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'aa1c12db-d580-55b2-b742-bb571416baf1','f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='aa1c12db-d580-55b2-b742-bb571416baf1' AND gt.achievement_id='f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'feea20aa-e83e-594a-8e5c-6766ab834860','Concentric runout','Concentric runout is the deviation of a disc''s recorded groove path from being truly concentric with its centre hole and spindle axis, so that as the disc rotates the groove traces an off-centre circle. Expressed as radial runout in geometrical tolerancing, this eccentricity causes the reproducing stylus to swing in and out once per revolution, producing a cyclic pitch variation heard as wow.','Runout means a spinning disc is not perfectly centered. The groove circle does not share the exact center of the spindle hole. So the groove drifts side to side as the disc turns. That makes the stylus swing once each turn, which you hear as a slow pitch wobble called wow.','The concept explains why an off-centre disc or hole produces audible wow, guiding technicians to detect and, where possible, correct eccentricity so transferred audio holds steady pitch.','During transfer a technician watches for a disc that visibly orbits or hears once-per-revolution wow, then re-centres the disc on the platter or applies eccentricity-correction software to reduce the pitch variation.','Disc Reproduction',ARRAY['Concentric cracking','Cut groove','Wow and flutter','Content migration','Content preservation']::text[],ARRAY['Blaming once-per-revolution wow on the turntable motor when an off-centre disc or hole is the real cause.','Assuming an eccentric disc cannot be improved, when re-centring or software correction often reduces the wow.','Confusing runout, an eccentric groove path in the plane of the disc, with a warp, which is vertical dishing of the surface.']::text[],ARRAY['Diagnosing cyclic pitch wobble while transferring a disc with an off-centre pressing or punched hole.','Re-centring a disc on the transfer platter to minimize eccentric-groove wow.','Applying eccentricity correction to a captured file after a disc''s hole proved off-centre.']::text[],'f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='feea20aa-e83e-594a-8e5c-6766ab834860' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Concentric runout'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'feea20aa-e83e-594a-8e5c-6766ab834860','f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='feea20aa-e83e-594a-8e5c-6766ab834860' AND gt.achievement_id='f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '56f37afa-87f1-56db-bb2f-2fc678f36061','Content Information','In the OAIS reference model (ISO 14721), Content Information is the set of information that is the original target of preservation, comprising the Content Data Object (the digital or physical object itself) together with its Representation Information, the data needed to render and interpret the object''s bit sequences into a meaningful form.','In the main preservation standard, Content Information has two parts. The first is the digital object you want to keep. The second is what you need to read and understand it. For example, an audio file plus a description of its format. Together they let future users make sense of the bits.','It defines exactly what an archive must preserve and understand, ensuring raw bits are kept together with the knowledge required to interpret them, so the content stays usable as technology changes.','When packaging an audio master into an Archival Information Package, an archivist bundles the BWF file (the Content Data Object) with format and technical documentation (the Representation Information) so the pairing forms complete Content Information.','OAIS Reference Model',ARRAY['Context information','Content preservation','Content migration','Coding history field','Controlled vocabulary']::text[],ARRAY['Preserving only the data object while discarding the Representation Information needed to interpret it later.','Confusing Content Information, which is object plus representation info, with the broader Archival Information Package that also holds preservation metadata.','Assuming the file format is self-describing, so no representation information needs to be recorded.']::text[],ARRAY['Assembling an OAIS-compliant package for a digitized sound recording.','Deciding what documentation must travel with an audio file to keep it readable in decades.','Explaining to a depositor why format specifications must accompany the deposited files.']::text[],'f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='56f37afa-87f1-56db-bb2f-2fc678f36061' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Content Information'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '56f37afa-87f1-56db-bb2f-2fc678f36061','f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='56f37afa-87f1-56db-bb2f-2fc678f36061' AND gt.achievement_id='f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'fcdb7fcc-0604-50cb-8699-8f371e350a9f','Content migration','Content migration is a digital preservation strategy in which content is periodically transferred to new file formats, storage media, or systems to keep it accessible as the originals become obsolete or degrade. Unlike bitstream copying alone, migration may transform the encoding while aiming to preserve the significant properties of the original content.','Content migration means moving digital content to newer formats or media before the old ones die out. Formats and disks do not last forever. So archives copy and convert the content on a schedule. The goal is to keep it readable while keeping its important qualities.','It exists to combat format and media obsolescence, ensuring content stays openable with current tools rather than becoming trapped in unsupported formats or failing hardware.','An archivist migrates a collection from an obsolete codec to a current preservation format, verifying with checksums and quality checks that the migrated files retain the original''s significant properties.','Preservation Strategy',ARRAY['Content preservation','Content Information','Checksum manifest','Cryptographic hash','Coding history field']::text[],ARRAY['Confusing migration, which may change the format, with simple bit-for-bit refreshing of storage media.','Migrating without validating that significant properties survived, so quality silently degrades over successive generations.','Delaying migration until the source format is already unsupported and the tools to read it are gone.']::text[],ARRAY['Planning a scheduled format migration for an audio repository facing codec obsolescence.','Moving preservation masters from ageing LTO tapes to a newer storage generation.','Converting legacy proprietary files to an open preservation format with fixity verification.']::text[],'f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='fcdb7fcc-0604-50cb-8699-8f371e350a9f' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Content migration'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'fcdb7fcc-0604-50cb-8699-8f371e350a9f','f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='fcdb7fcc-0604-50cb-8699-8f371e350a9f' AND gt.achievement_id='f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'fe1da702-9bcc-50ec-ba8f-a5b8eb0c6fe4','Content preservation','Content preservation is the set of managed activities that keep the intellectual content of a resource accessible, authentic, and understandable over the long term, independent of any single carrier or format. It draws on strategies such as migration, emulation, fixity monitoring, and metadata management, prioritizing the information conveyed over the specific physical or digital object that carries it.','Content preservation means keeping the actual information usable for the long term. It cares about the message, not just one disk or file. Archives copy, convert, and check the content over time. That way people can still open it and trust it many years later.','It ensures that what a recording or document conveys survives even as media decay and formats change, shifting the goal from saving a fragile carrier to sustaining the accessible, authentic content itself.','A technician supports content preservation by digitizing at-risk analog media, storing multiple verified copies, monitoring fixity, and migrating formats before they become obsolete.','Preservation Strategy',ARRAY['Content migration','Content Information','Context information','Checksum manifest','Cryptographic hash']::text[],ARRAY['Equating content preservation with a single backup, ignoring format obsolescence and long-term access.','Focusing on saving the original carrier at the expense of transferring the endangered content in time.','Neglecting metadata, so preserved files survive but lose the context needed to understand them.']::text[],ARRAY['Building a preservation plan for a decaying magnetic tape collection.','Justifying digitization of obsolete formats to protect their content.','Setting a policy of multiple, geographically separate, fixity-checked copies.']::text[],'f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='fe1da702-9bcc-50ec-ba8f-a5b8eb0c6fe4' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Content preservation'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'fe1da702-9bcc-50ec-ba8f-a5b8eb0c6fe4','f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='fe1da702-9bcc-50ec-ba8f-a5b8eb0c6fe4' AND gt.achievement_id='f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'c2db3ecd-5fdb-51bb-aa1e-89aa96c79cc0','Context information','In the OAIS reference model (ISO 14721), Context Information is a component of the Preservation Description Information that documents how the Content Information relates to its environment and to other Content Information, for example why it was created and its relationships to companion objects or alternative versions. It records the surrounding circumstances needed to fully situate and interpret the preserved content.','In the main preservation standard, Context Information explains how a preserved object relates to other things. It records why the object was made and what it connects to. For example, which recording session or series it belongs to. This background helps future users understand it.','It preserves the relationships and circumstances around content so future users can understand not just the object itself but how and why it fits within a larger collection or purpose.','When cataloguing a digitized recording, an archivist records context such as the originating project, related takes, and links to companion documents so the file''s place in the collection stays clear.','OAIS Reference Model',ARRAY['Content Information','Content preservation','Controlled vocabulary','Coding history field','Content migration']::text[],ARRAY['Confusing Context Information, which covers relationships and circumstances, with Representation Information, which explains how to render the bits.','Recording only the object and omitting why it exists or what it relates to.','Assuming context can be reconstructed later, after the people and records that hold it are gone.']::text[],ARRAY['Documenting how a single tape relates to a multi-session recording project.','Linking a digitized interview to its release and companion transcript.','Capturing the reason a recording was created during accessioning.']::text[],'f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='c2db3ecd-5fdb-51bb-aa1e-89aa96c79cc0' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Context information'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'c2db3ecd-5fdb-51bb-aa1e-89aa96c79cc0','f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='c2db3ecd-5fdb-51bb-aa1e-89aa96c79cc0' AND gt.achievement_id='f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '32afbbd6-e87d-52b4-b891-5d4cb7fc4b8a','Controlled vocabulary','A controlled vocabulary is a curated, standardized list of preferred terms, together with their permitted relationships and synonym mappings, used to index, describe, and retrieve resources consistently, as set out in ANSI/NISO Z39.19. By enforcing one authorized form for each concept and mapping variants to it, it removes ambiguity from metadata; thesauri and authority files such as the Getty Art & Architecture Thesaurus are common examples.','A controlled vocabulary is an approved list of words used to label things the same way every time. It picks one official term for each idea. Different words for the same thing all point to that one term. This makes searching and cataloguing consistent and reliable.','It exists to make metadata consistent and searchable by ensuring everyone uses the same agreed terms, so related items are found together despite differing natural-language wording.','A cataloguer assigns subject and genre terms from a controlled vocabulary such as the Getty AAT or Library of Congress Subject Headings, choosing preferred terms instead of free text so the collection is uniformly searchable.','Archival Metadata',ARRAY['Context information','Content Information','Coding history field','Content preservation','Content migration']::text[],ARRAY['Inventing free-text keywords instead of using the vocabulary''s preferred term, which fragments retrieval.','Confusing a controlled vocabulary with a plain keyword list that lacks defined relationships and synonym control.','Applying terms inconsistently across cataloguers because the authorized forms were not enforced.']::text[],ARRAY['Selecting subject headings for a digitized audio collection.','Mapping variant spellings of a performer''s name to one authorized form.','Adopting the Getty AAT to standardize genre terms across a media catalogue.']::text[],'f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='32afbbd6-e87d-52b4-b891-5d4cb7fc4b8a' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Controlled vocabulary'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '32afbbd6-e87d-52b4-b891-5d4cb7fc4b8a','f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='32afbbd6-e87d-52b4-b891-5d4cb7fc4b8a' AND gt.achievement_id='f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '9d91dbee-ce87-5bb3-8acc-db303f434298','Core swelling','Core swelling is a deterioration of moulded celluloid phonograph cylinders in which the internal plaster-of-Paris or cardboard core absorbs moisture and expands, placing outward stress on the bonded celluloid shell. The dimensional change can split or crack the celluloid and alter the cylinder''s fit on the playback mandrel, threatening the recorded surface.','Some old cylinder records have a hard core inside a thin plastic shell. If the core takes in damp air, it swells and pushes outward. That pressure can crack the shell that holds the sound. It can also make the cylinder too tight to sit on the player.','Understanding core swelling helps an archivist store cylinders in stable, moderate humidity and spot early stress cracks, so the fragile recorded surface is protected before damage becomes irreversible.','Before playback an operator checks a moulded cylinder for a swollen or cracked core, avoids forcing a tight cylinder onto the mandrel, and controls storage humidity to slow further expansion.','Media Degradation',ARRAY['Cylinder bore deformation','Concentric cracking','Content migration','Content preservation','Cut groove']::text[],ARRAY['UNSAFE: Forcing a swollen cylinder onto the mandrel, which can split the brittle celluloid and injure the handler with sharp fragments; ease off and reassess instead.','Storing cylinders in damp conditions, which accelerates core moisture uptake and swelling.','Mistaking early core-stress cracks for harmless surface marks and playing the cylinder anyway.']::text[],ARRAY['Assessing a collection of moulded cylinders for core damage before transfer.','Setting storage humidity to protect cylinders with plaster cores.','Deciding a swollen cylinder needs specialist handling rather than routine playback.']::text[],'f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='9d91dbee-ce87-5bb3-8acc-db303f434298' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Core swelling'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
