SELECT '253b4b75-0905-55da-aab5-7dceb07c1754','f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='253b4b75-0905-55da-aab5-7dceb07c1754' AND gt.achievement_id='f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'f307e173-5a6f-5fa1-89f4-37f009d4b930','Pinch effect','A source of second-harmonic distortion in the replay of laterally-cut discs. Because the recording (cutting) stylus is chisel- or V-shaped while the reproducing stylus is round, the groove is narrower at the peaks of lateral modulation; as the round stylus passes these points it is squeezed and forced to rise vertically twice per modulation cycle, producing an unwanted vertical motion at twice the recorded frequency.','A record''s groove is cut with a sharp, wedge-shaped tool but played with a rounded tip. Where the wiggle in the groove is sharpest, the groove gets narrower. The round tip cannot fit, so it is pushed upward. This up-and-down jiggle adds a false tone and distortion.','Understanding the pinch effect explains a built-in distortion of lateral disc reproduction and why vertical stylus motion on a mono disc carries mostly unwanted signal, guiding stylus choice and playback technique that minimise it.','A transfer engineer selects an appropriately profiled and sized stylus and a well-aligned pickup to reduce pinch-effect distortion, and understands why summing to mono can cancel some vertical artefacts on a mono lateral disc.','Disc Reproduction',ARRAY['Tracing distortion','Stylus selection','Lateral recording','Vertical-cut disc','Preservation transfer']::text[],ARRAY['Blaming pinch-effect distortion on a worn disc or dirty groove; it is inherent to round-stylus replay of a chisel-cut groove, not damage.','Assuming a larger or sharper stylus always sounds better; stylus size and profile must match the groove to limit pinch effect and tracing distortion without riding worn walls.','Ignoring vertical stylus motion on a mono lateral disc; that motion is largely noise and pinch-effect artefact and can be reduced by taking the mono sum.']::text[],ARRAY['Choosing a stylus profile to transfer a coarse-groove 78 with minimal second-harmonic distortion.','Explaining audible distortion on loud passages of a mono disc as pinch effect rather than groove damage.','Configuring a mono sum during transfer to suppress vertical pinch artefacts.']::text[],'f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='f307e173-5a6f-5fa1-89f4-37f009d4b930' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Pinch effect'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'f307e173-5a6f-5fa1-89f4-37f009d4b930','f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='f307e173-5a6f-5fa1-89f4-37f009d4b930' AND gt.achievement_id='f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '801611d8-f43b-5272-bbe9-18ab09c1af6d','Plasticizer loss','The gradual depletion of a plasticizer from a polymer coating or carrier — most importantly the castor-oil plasticizer in the nitrocellulose lacquer of instantaneous (acetate) discs — through evaporation and hydrolysis. Loss of the plasticizer makes the coating shrink and embrittle, so it cracks, flakes and finally delaminates from the rigid core; the exuded breakdown products appear as a white fatty-acid (palmitic and stearic) bloom.','Plasticizer is an oil added to a coating to keep it soft and bendy. Over time it escapes or breaks down. Without it the coating shrinks and turns brittle. On old lacquer discs this makes the surface crack and peel away from the hard base.','Recognising plasticizer loss lets a conservator predict which discs are about to fail catastrophically and transfer them first, because once the coating cracks and delaminates the recording is lost for good.','An engineer flags discs showing shrinkage, cupping, hairline cracks or white bloom as suffering plasticizer loss and moves them to immediate digitization before delamination occurs.','Disc Degradation',ARRAY['Palmitic-acid exudation','Delamination','Lacquer disc','Preservation triage','Particulate occlusion']::text[],ARRAY['UNSAFE: Attempting to ''restore'' flexibility with oils, alcohols or solvents; these attack the lacquer and accelerate destruction, so degraded lacquer discs should be transferred rather than chemically treated except by qualified conservators.','Assuming cold storage reverses the damage; low temperature slows further loss but cannot restore lost plasticizer or re-adhere a delaminated coating.','Storing lacquer discs in warm, humid conditions, which speeds hydrolysis and plasticizer loss.']::text[],ARRAY['A batch of 16-inch radio transcription discs shows shrinkage cracks radiating from the centre hole.','Palmitic-acid bloom on a lacquer disc signals ongoing plasticizer loss and triggers urgent transfer.','Cold, dry archival storage is chosen to slow plasticizer loss across a lacquer-disc collection.']::text[],'f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='801611d8-f43b-5272-bbe9-18ab09c1af6d' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Plasticizer loss'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '801611d8-f43b-5272-bbe9-18ab09c1af6d','f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='801611d8-f43b-5272-bbe9-18ab09c1af6d' AND gt.achievement_id='f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '080b76de-75dc-5ece-94c2-e1d6aabdc4fc','Pre-grooved disc','A recording disc manufactured with a continuous spiral guide groove already formed in its surface before any signal is recorded, so that the writing element is fed and steadied by the pre-existing groove. Analog instantaneous, embossing and dictation blanks carried a pre-cut or pre-embossed groove so a recorder lacking a precise lead-screw feed could still track; recordable optical discs such as CD-R and DVD-R use a moulded ''pregroove'', often wobbled to carry timing information, to steer the writing laser.','Most recordable discs already have a spiral groove built in before you record. This ready-made groove guides the recorder as it works. On old home discs a needle followed the groove. On a recordable CD a laser follows a similar built-in groove.','The pre-formed groove provides tracking and feed, letting a simpler recorder lay down a usable spiral without a precise lead-screw mechanism, and in optical media it gives the laser a physical track and timing reference.','When identifying an obsolete home or dictation recording, an archivist may find a pre-grooved instantaneous disc and must match playback speed and stylus to the pre-formed groove to recover it cleanly.','Disc Formats',ARRAY['Instantaneous disc','Lacquer disc','Embossed groove','Pinch effect','Preservation transfer']::text[],ARRAY['Assuming every disc''s groove was cut during recording; a pre-grooved blank already carried a spiral guide groove before any sound was recorded.','Using a standard microgroove stylus on a coarse pre-grooved home disc; the stylus and speed must match the actual groove or playback fails and the fragile surface is damaged.','Treating a fragile embossed pre-grooved disc as robust; embossed and home-recorded discs wear or deform easily and need careful, minimal handling.']::text[],ARRAY['Cataloguing a mid-century home recording found on a pre-grooved instantaneous disc.','Transferring a dictation disc whose pre-formed groove was embossed rather than cut.','Selecting a stylus to fit the pre-formed groove of an obsolete recording blank.']::text[],'f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='080b76de-75dc-5ece-94c2-e1d6aabdc4fc' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Pre-grooved disc'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '080b76de-75dc-5ece-94c2-e1d6aabdc4fc','f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='080b76de-75dc-5ece-94c2-e1d6aabdc4fc' AND gt.achievement_id='f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '0666bb63-40d5-5fba-9838-4349b9588d4c','Preservation Description Information','In the OAIS reference model (ISO 14721), the information that, together with Representation Information, is necessary to adequately preserve a Content Data Object. It comprises five categories — Provenance, Context, Reference, Fixity and Access Rights Information — documenting where the object came from and its history, how it relates to other objects, how it is uniquely identified, how its integrity is verified, and what access restrictions apply.','When an archive stores a digital file, the file alone is not enough. Preservation Description Information is the record kept beside it. It says where the file came from and how it links to other items. It also gives its name and how to check it is unchanged. Finally, it says who may use it.','It packages the metadata an archive must keep so that a stored object stays understandable, trustworthy and usable over time, independent of the people and systems that created it.','When building an Archival Information Package, a digital archivist records checksums (fixity), transfer history (provenance), identifiers (reference), relationships (context) and rights so the package meets OAIS requirements.','Preservation Metadata',ARRAY['Preservation provenance','Fixity','Persistent identifier','Preservation normalization','Metadata']::text[],ARRAY['Confusing Preservation Description Information with Representation Information; the former documents history, identity, integrity and rights, while the latter tells you how to render the bits.','Recording only descriptive catalogue metadata and omitting fixity and provenance, which leaves the object unverifiable and its history unknown.','Treating it as optional; under OAIS it is required for adequate preservation, not a nice-to-have.']::text[],ARRAY['Assembling an Archival Information Package for a digitized tape collection and attaching checksums, source history and rights.','Auditing a repository against OAIS to confirm all five categories are captured.','Using recorded provenance to prove a preservation master is the authentic transfer of an original.']::text[],'f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='0666bb63-40d5-5fba-9838-4349b9588d4c' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Preservation Description Information'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '0666bb63-40d5-5fba-9838-4349b9588d4c','f2442389-2379-4010-8b5a-6835773b85c7',true
